export const skill =
  "---\nname: devenv\ndescription: Configure and run a devenv project in this session. Use when the project has or needs devenv.nix — packages, language options, env, services, scripts, tasks and tests — or when pinning devenv.yaml and devenv.lock.\n---\n\n# devenv\n\n`devenv.nix` describes a project's developer environment: tools on `PATH`, environment variables, services, tasks and tests, pinned by `devenv.yaml` and `devenv.lock`. Paseo prepares the nearest ancestor project and applies it to this session, so the environment is already active. `PASEO_DEVENV_ROOT` names the project root the environment came from and `PASEO_DEVENV_STATUS` its state.\n\nRun project commands directly (`pnpm test`, `jq`, a language toolchain). Do not activate the environment a second time: do not wrap commands in `devenv shell -- <command>`, do not start an interactive `devenv shell`, and do not call `devenv direnv-export`; the session already evaluated the project. Commands in another project directory still use this session's launch environment, so use a separate agent for another project.\n\n## Set up or edit the project\n\nRun `devenv init` only when the project has no `devenv.nix`, and preserve the project's existing configuration when adding devenv.\n\n`devenv.nix` is a Nix module. Prefer declarative options over shell hooks:\n\n- `packages = [ ... ];` for individual tools;\n- `languages.<lang>.enable = true;` for language toolchains;\n- `env.NAME = \"value\";` for environment variables;\n- `services.<service>.enable = true;` for services;\n- `scripts.<name>` for project scripts, which devenv exposes on `PATH`;\n- `tasks.\"name:exec\" = { ... };` and `processes` for repeatable and long-running work;\n- `enterShell` only for what declarative options cannot express, kept short.\n\nSearch before adding anything so option names and attribute paths are exact. When this session has the `devenv` MCP server, use its `search_packages` and `search_options` tools rather than guessing or shelling out; they query the project's pinned inputs. That server runs `devenv mcp` in the project root and refuses an untrusted project, so fall back to `devenv search <term>` when the tools are absent, and trust what the installed devenv version reports over remembered option names.\n\n## Keep inputs pinned\n\n`devenv.yaml` declares inputs and `devenv.lock` records their resolved revisions. Keep both committed together, and use `devenv update [input]` for a targeted refresh; never hand-edit `devenv.lock`. Inspect the lock diff before keeping it, and report a changed nixpkgs revision instead of silently rebuilding the world. Human shells may activate the project through `.envrc` with `devenv direnvrc` and `direnv allow`; that is separate from this session's trust.\n\n## Run tasks, services and tests\n\n- `devenv tasks list`, then `devenv tasks run <task>`; add `--mode single` to skip dependencies.\n- `devenv test` runs the `enterTest` checks.\n- When the `devenv` MCP server is available, drive services and processes through its tools — `list_processes`, `get_process_status`, `get_process_logs`, `start_process`, `stop_process`, `restart_process` — instead of reaching for `devenv up` or `devenv processes` by hand.\n- Run foreground supervisors such as `devenv up` as managed background work with cleanup when done; a declared service needs a running supervisor before a test that expects it can pass.\n- Once the environment is active, the project's own commands are the fastest verification: build, lint and test directly.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- Nix can build for minutes on a cold cache and needs network access. Prefer targeted checks over building everything, and do not run two long Nix builds at once.\n- Preparation evaluates `devenv.nix` with the daemon user's operating-system permissions. Configure only projects the user owns, and tell the user when a request would evaluate an untrusted tree.\n";
